-- title:
--   A ball of radius below the cone gap is contained in the cone
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ be a unit vector, $\lVert v \rVert = 1$, and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$. Write $\tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ for the open half-cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$, and, for $p \in E$ with components $a = \langle v, p\rangle$ along the axis and $b = \lVert p - a v\rVert$ across it, write
--
--   $$\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) \;=\; a \sin\vartheta - b \cos\vartheta$$
--
--   for the signed distance from $p$ to the boundary of the cone. If $\rho \in \mathbb{R}$ satisfies $\rho < \mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p)$, then
--
--   $$\bar B(p,\rho) \subseteq \tilde V(v,\vartheta),$$
--
--   the closed ball of radius $\rho$ about $p$ lying entirely inside the cone.
--
--   This is the quantitative form of "$p$ is well inside the cone" used throughout Sections 4 and 5: it converts a single scalar estimate on the gap into a whole neighbourhood of room, which is what allows an entire cube or block, rather than a single point, to be swallowed by a cone.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The inequality on $\rho$ is strict, and no sign hypothesis is imposed on $\rho$; for $\rho < 0$ the closed ball is empty and the conclusion is trivially true. The statement is proved in an arbitrary real inner product space, not only in $\mathbb{R}^d$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L107-L115

